"L" <L_1_L_0@yahoo.com> wrote:
>I assumed that Baalzebub's stare is _always_ piercing, just as
>Yeenoghu's is always confusing.
>What does early D&D say concerning the nature of Baalzebub's gaze,
>anyway?

AD&D Monster Manual, page 21, Baalzebul (Arch-devil):
"His glance causes _fear_ and _weakness_ (saving throw versus magic or
collapse in trembling until 1-4 melee rounds after the gaze is lifted)
whenever he directly stares at any person."

Note in particular that this, unlike the gaze of a medusa or basilisk,
is not subject to reflection; it's not that his eyes shoot beams of
fear-radiation, but more simply that having an arch-devil staring at
you is bloody terrifying.

It also says that Yeenoghu's confusion attack is caused by the third
ball of his flail :-)
